WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.220
- [Kim] Hey, this is
Kim, from Khan Academy

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.740
and today, I'm learning about Article IV

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.510
of the U.S. Constitution.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.410
Article IV lays out the nuts and bolts

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.100
of how Federalism, the
system of shared governance

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.350
between states and the federal

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.210
government works in practice.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.630
Article IV has four sections.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.320
The first two, the Full
Faith and Credit clause

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.090
and the Privileges and Immunities clause

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.630
talk about how states will
treat each other's citizens

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.580
as well as they treat the
citizens of their own states.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.800
Then the third section
is an Admissions clause

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.590
discussing how new states
will be added to the Union

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.830
and the fourth section
is the Guarantee clause,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.980
which guarantees every state in the Union

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.980
a Republican form of government.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.520
To learn more about Article IV

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.600
I sought out the help of two experts.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.440
Erin Hawley is an
Associate Professor of Law

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.940
at the University of Missouri.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.170
Her scholarship focuses
on the federal courts,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.580
and she teaches Constitutional Litigation,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.870
Tax Policy, and Agricultural Law.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.950
Professor Gabriel Chin is the Director

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.460
of Clinical Legal Education
at the UC Davis School of Law.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.050
He's a teacher and scholar
of Immigration Law,

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.550
criminal procedure, and race and law.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.700
Professor Hawley, can you
take us a little bit through

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.230
why the framers included Article IV?

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.917
What was its purpose?

00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:15.630
- [Professor Hawley] The
founders of the Constitution

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.010
were very concerned that
the federal government

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.630
be one of limited powers

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:22.850
and, because of that, they saw the states

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.900
as having an active and
critical role in placing

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.930
a check on the federal government.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.860
So, we've got the three branches

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.530
and their own checks and balances

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.320
and then we've got the federal government

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.700
and the state government
also playing a role in

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.440
checking and balancing each other.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.750
They wanted to establish a

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.700
strong central government, but also to

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.610
ensure that it didn't have too much power,

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.130
and the states were
critical to this effort.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.750
Also, they very much wanted the states

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.790
to act collectively, not individually.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.940
As you'll recall, the states
had not been doing so well

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.500
under the loose Articles
of the Confederation.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.630
They'd sort of been going it alone

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.000
on critical issues like trade and defense

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.910
to the detriment of the Union.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.370
So, Article IV is also

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.731
a, sort of, key to making
sure that the states act,

00:02:05.731 --> 00:02:07.410
sort of, as a unified whole,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.240
rather than going it alone.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.650
- [Professor Chin] It's
one country made up

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.360
of diverse states, and

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.130
if you prefer the way

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.823
things are done in Nevada,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.930
you can move there.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.540
And if you think that some
other state has a better set

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.540
of answers to the problems
of modern life, you can move.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.710
What the Full Faith and
Credit clause and the

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.540
Privileges and Immunities
clause are designed to do

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.740
is to facilitate transactions,
to facilitate moving,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:44.740
to facilitate communications and commerce

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.930
and trade and travel, among the states.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.270
But that doesn't mean that what's going on

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.120
in each of the states can't
be very, very different.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.400
- [Kim] We often think
of checks and balances

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.160
as being something that was designed

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.580
to be kind of horizontal.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.560
That the Legislature
and the Executive branch

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.650
and the Judicial branch,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.860
just kind of all at the same
level checking each other.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.140
But there's also kind of this

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.680
vertical checks and
balances happening, too,

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.480
between the power of
the federal government,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.313
the power of the states,

00:03:12.313 --> 00:03:14.706
and the power of the local governments.

00:03:14.706 --> 00:03:17.100
- [Professor Hawley]
Absolutely; we've got the, the,

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.080
three branches and their
checks and balances.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.710
But we also have a
strong central government

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.390
that's checked in large part by

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.190
strong, independent, sovereign governments

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.490
in each of the 50 states and these states

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.650
traditionally have what
are known as Police Powers,

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.190
so they have a lot of
inherent authority to govern

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:38.250
the people in those states,
subject to federal law.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.120
But it really does sort
of place a check on

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.250
federal authority, and I
think this is precisely

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:44.800
what the framers wanted
because they did want

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.360
a strong government, but
they also were very much

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.440
of the view that states were important,

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:51.730
that their own states were important

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:55.230
and they didn't want to
lose that in the new,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.730
new Constitution and
new federal government.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.040
- [Kim] There are four sections

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.030
in Article IV, and the first section

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.810
deals with Full Faith and Credit.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.120
And it says Full Faith
and Credit shall be given

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.740
in each State to the public Acts,

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.400
Records, and judicial
Proceedings of every other State.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.260
What does Full Faith and
Credit actually mean?

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.290
- [Professor Chin] It's designed to make,

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.650
in a certain sense all of the states

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:22.150
of the United States
part of a single system.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.470
And, so, Full Faith and Credit means that

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.370
a court judgment, for
example, in one state,

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:33.335
will be recognized in every state.

00:04:33.335 --> 00:04:35.000
- [Professor Hawley] If you
have a valid judgment in

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.940
New York, for example, and
you move to California,

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.310
the California courts are
required to give effect

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.232
to that judgment, to that
state court judgment,

00:04:42.232 --> 00:04:44.980
so long as it was validly issued.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.400
There was a federal statute known as the

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.750
Defense of Marriage Act, or DOMA,

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.990
that was passed under President Clinton,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.330
and recently the Supreme Court

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:56.130
struck that down as unconstitutional.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:58.350
So now under Full Faith and
Credit if you're married in one

00:04:58.350 --> 00:04:59.935
state, you're married in
another state as well.

00:04:59.935 --> 00:05:02.233
- [Professor Chin] You can
see the kinds of problems

00:05:02.233 --> 00:05:04.400
that would exist if states

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.690
didn't honor the legal decisions

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.010
that were made by other states

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:14.010
such as, who's married or who's divorced?

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.920
Or who owns a particular
piece of property?

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.840
Or whether a particular child

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.980
is going to be in the custody of one

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.910
parent rather than another?

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:28.660
And the Full Faith and Credit
clause is designed to say

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.410
in order for our system

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.790
to work, as a unified whole,

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:37.010
while it's true that the
courts of Georgia are

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.280
distinct from the Courts
of New York, et cetera,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.200
they're separate systems, but they have

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.780
to treat the work that each
other does with respect.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.470
- [Kim] If we move on to section two,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.980
this says that the Citizens of each State

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.594
shall be entitled to all

00:05:54.594 --> 00:05:59.594
Privileges and Immunities of
Citizens in the several States.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.913
What are these Privileges and Immunities?

00:06:02.877 --> 00:06:05.000
- [Professor Hawley] The
Privileges and Immunities clause

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.630
has been one that's subject to

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.380
a number of, sort of, debate,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.910
in the courts and the academic literature.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.760
But, basically, Privileges
and Immunities have been

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.070
construed to be those sorts of things

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.930
that would go with citizenship.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.856
So, the right to travel, for example,

00:06:20.856 --> 00:06:22.300
is a Privilege and Immunity.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.980
Those sorts of things.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.040
- [Professor Chin] Occasionally
in American history,

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.970
there have been moments where states

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.370
didn't wanna let citizens of
other states come through,

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.430
so during the Depression

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.550
there was an effort by some states to

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:41.550
limit the migration of people
from out of state to in state.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:45.400
And the Supreme Court said
that's not permissible.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.750
It also protects the right to travel.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:51.560
In 1999, the Supreme Court
dealt with a case called

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.930
Saenz vs. Roe, and what
that case was about is that

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.920
California had relatively generous

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:03.320
welfare benefits and California wanted to

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:04.270
set up its law

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.140
in such a way that it
wouldn't encourage people

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:08.420
from other states to move to California

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.100
just to get the welfare benefits.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.640
And, so, what they did is, they said,

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:14.170
that if you don't live in California,

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.680
if you're moving from out of state

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.200
and you apply for welfare benefits,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.910
then we're gonna give you the welfare

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:21.260
benefits that you would have gotten

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:22.910
in your state for the first year.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:25.510
We're not gonna give you the
higher California benefits,

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.110
we're gonna give you
whatever you would've gotten

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:29.680
where you came from.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.300
That's unconstitutional
and the Supreme Court said

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.440
that violates the Privileges
and Immunities clause.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.121
We're one county, one nation.

00:07:38.121 --> 00:07:41.530
People are allowed to cross
the borders whenever they want.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:43.360
- [Kim] Interesting, but
there's this second part of it

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:46.530
that says: No person held to Service

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.080
or Labour in one State,
under the Laws thereof,

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.500
escaping into another,
shall, in Consequence

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.600
of any Law or Regulation therein,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.180
be discharged from Service or Labour.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.577
What is that all about?

00:07:57.577 --> 00:07:59.550
- [Professor Hawley] That is
one of the most unfortunate,

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:02.580
probably the most unfortunate
clauses in our Constitution

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.360
and it's known as the
Fugitive Slave clause

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.960
and that title is pretty descriptive.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.890
If you have a slave, who is validly owned,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.320
as it were in those days,
under one states law and that

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.340
person escaped to a free state,

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.200
this clause gave the owner the right to

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:24.220
reclaim that slave and put them back

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.290
into, sort of, ownership
in their own state.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:27.370
- [Professor Chin] It is a sneaky way

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:29.910
of talking about slavery and

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:33.750
this compromise with slavery was necessary

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.510
to create the United States.

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:39.920
Slavery, the word, isn't
used in the Constitution.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.920
They only use these euphemisms,

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:47.580
these, sort of, complicated
circumlocutions,

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:49.637
and there's an argument that
there's a reason for that.

00:08:49.637 --> 00:08:51.400
And the reason for that is

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.970
that a lot of the framers
of the Constitution

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.630
didn't support slavery, opposed slavery.

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.020
They did think that it
was important to have

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:01.650
a United States and to
have a Constitution,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.270
so they wanted to,

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:06.070
to do what was necessary to achieve that.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:08.240
But they did the absolute
minimum and they did it

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.500
in such a way

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:13.760
that, consciously, doesn't recognize

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.170
and legitimize the institution of slavery.

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:19.660
- [Kim] All right, so, then we get into

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.630
New States and Territories.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:25.690
I think it was very forward-looking

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:28.240
of the framers to recognize that

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.140
new states wanna join the Union,

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:31.970
and then to provide a process for that.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:33.170
Can you tell us a little bit about

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:34.946
what that process was like?

00:09:34.946 --> 00:09:37.500
- [Professor Hawley] Article
IV, Section Three, Clause One

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.180
provides the process for
admitting new states.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.580
Again, it recognizes that new states might

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.430
want to join the Union,

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.990
and it gives a wide latitude to Congress

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:48.700
for admitting new states.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.410
Basically the process was that

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.680
a territory would indicate to the Congress

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.370
that it wished to become a state.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:56.600
Then they would submit a constitution

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.520
and Congress would approve the new state.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:01.320
But the Constitution,
itself, in Article IV, places

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:03.000
some limitationa on that.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.420
Some of the Eastern states were concerned

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:07.180
that the large Western territories

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.670
might become too influential.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.500
They had a couple of
provisions that no new state

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:14.530
could be created out of an old state,

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.580
nor could parts of states be combined

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.720
to form a new state
unless there was consent

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.400
from all of the involved states.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:24.790
- [Professor Chin] And it's
not so clear that, that

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:29.580
purchase of territory
from foreign governments

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:34.140
was a power that was granted
in the Constitution to anyone.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:35.940
Thomas Jefferson, who was the President

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.980
at the time of the Louisiana Purchase,

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:41.840
had doubts that the Louisiana
Purchase was Constitutional.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.190
He thought that it was
a great idea to purchase

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.610
all that land, from France,

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:47.950
but he thought that it would require

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:50.850
a Constitutional Amendment
for the United States

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:51.880
to have that power.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.960
This is different from
Texas joining the Union.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.040
But the House and the Senate didn't have

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.110
the same qualms that
President Jefferson did

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:04.450
and, so, they agreed

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:07.940
to approve the Louisiana
Purchase and to fund it.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:11.280
When the bill got to Thomas
Jefferson's desk, he signed it.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:12.960
- [Kim] Moving on to Section Four,

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.950
there's this promise, here,
that the federal government

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:19.000
will guarantee every
state a Republican Form

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.370
of Government, and shall
protect each of them

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.020
against invasion or domestic violence.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:26.117
What does this mean?

00:11:26.117 --> 00:11:27.670
- [Professor Hawley] We
see here, in Article IV,

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.490
the end of Article IV, a
really, sort of, famous

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:34.290
and important promise to each
of the individual states.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.560
The federal government is promising

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:39.040
to basically aid them in

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.500
keeping a Republican form of government.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.620
As we'll remember, Benjamin
Franklin famously said,

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.828
We've given you a republic
if you can keep it.

00:11:47.828 --> 00:11:48.780
(Kim laughs)

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.260
- [Professor Chin] When
we talk about invasion,

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.960
when Section Four talks about invasion,

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.010
it's pretty clear that what
they're talking about is

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:57.380
invasion by some hostile state.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.070
And, of course, during this period,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.630
there was continuing
conflict with England.

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.840
And the idea is that if New York

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:07.990
or South Carolina gets

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.840
invaded by England, that's not
just that particular state's

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:14.830
problem, it's the nation's problem and

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.230
the national government has an obligation

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.510
to protect against invasion.

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:22.810
- [Kim] And I imagine it
also prevents someone from,

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:25.460
for example, declaring
themselves the king of Maryland.

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.210
- [Professor Chin] The Republican Form of

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.030
Government clause does that.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.950
It leaves the details to the states,

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:35.110
but the basic idea is
that there has; the, the,

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:37.980
the concept of Republican government

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.330
that's embodied in Article
IV is majority rule.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:46.410
That even if there were
free and fair elections,

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.270
the state could not choose

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:51.250
to have military or hereditary government.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.880
Even if everybody in
California got together

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.380
and said, you know, what
we really need is a,

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:00.620
a good family that
generation after generation

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:02.780
will lead us, and they will be

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.470
the, the kings and queens of California,

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:08.270
and this is what we want

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:10.710
and so, we're going to
amend our Constitution

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:14.910
to provide for hereditary
leadership in our state.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.480
And we're gonna find
somebody great and this

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:17.370
is what we wanna do.

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:19.900
Article IV would say,

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:21.450
we can't choose to do that,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.680
even through Democratic means.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:25.250
- [Kim] As we've learned, Article IV

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:27.720
requires states to give
Full Faith and Credit

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:29.920
to the legal proceedings of other states,

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.050
and to treat the citizens of other states

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.310
as well as they treat their own citizens.

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:37.280
It provides a process for
adding new states into the mix

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.710
and guarantees a Republican
Form of Government

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:41.660
to the citizens of all the states.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:44.760
Article IV binds the
United States together,

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.760
so it's not just a collection
of independent states,

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.660
but rather a unified nation.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:51.330
To learn more about Article IV,

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:53.230
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:55.800
interactive Constitution
and Khan Academy's

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.253
resources on U.S. government and politics.

